\title[Cut-Indexed Defects]{Cut-Indexed Defects: A Finite Cut-Data Theory of the
Singleton Bound, its Entropic Refinement, and its Quantum Avatar}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We isolate the purely finite, order-theoretic content of the notion of \emph{bond
dimension across a cut} in a tensor network, and show that it suffices to prove a
family of Singleton-type inequalities indexed by cuts. A \emph{cut datum} on $n$
sites with local dimension $q$ is a function $r$ from subsets of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ to
the natural numbers, together with a total dimension $\tot$, subject to three
axioms: $r(\varnothing)\le 1$, monotonicity, and one-site growth
$r(S\cup\{a\})\le q\,r(S)$. Calling a cut datum \emph{$d$-resolving} when
$n-|S|<d$ forces $r(S)=\tot$, we prove the \emph{cut-wise Singleton inequality}
$\tot\le q^{\,k-|S|}r(S)$ with $k:=n+1-d$ and $|S|\le k$, whose specialisation at
$S=\varnothing$ is the classical Singleton bound, and a \emph{rigidity theorem}:
saturation $\tot=q^{k}$ forces $r(S)=q^{|S|}$ at every cut below the plateau.
Instantiating at the cut rank of a finite codebook recovers the Singleton bound,
the theorem that every $k$ coordinates of an MDS code form an information set, and
an exact balanced-fibre count. We then build the \emph{entropic mirror} of the
axiom system for the Shannon entropy $H(S)$ of the marginal induced on a cut, and
deduce the \emph{entropic cut-wise Singleton inequality}
$\log|C|\le H(S)+(k-|S|)\log q$, which implies the counting version and is strictly
sharper whenever the cut marginal is non-uniform; the key analytic input is a
relativised grouping inequality proved here. We show the entropy profile of an MDS
code is exactly the plateau $H(S)=\min(|S|,k)\log q$, and that the vanishing of the
entropic defect at a single cut characterises the MDS property. Finally, promoting
a codebook to the uniform superposition state, we prove a \emph{quantum cut-wise
Singleton inequality} $E(S)\le\min(|S|,k)\log q$, saturated exactly for
$|S|\le\min(k,d-1)$, where the reduced density matrix is maximally mixed. A
three-bit example shows this guard is sharp: the quantum profile is a \emph{tent},
forced down by global purity, whereas the classical profile is a monotone
\emph{staircase}. A five-word code shows the cut rank is not submodular, in
contrast with the cut entropy.
\end{abstract}

